## Objective

Build a Flutter/Dart state model using a sealed hierarchy and create focused examples of the major class modifiers.

## Step 1 — Create a Sealed State Hierarchy

```dart
sealed class ProductState {}

class ProductInitial extends ProductState {}

class ProductLoading extends ProductState {}

class ProductLoaded extends ProductState {
  final List<String> products;

  ProductLoaded(this.products);
}

class ProductError extends ProductState {
  final String message;

  ProductError(this.message);
}
```

## Step 2 — Exhaustive Switch

```dart
String stateMessage(ProductState state) {
  return switch (state) {
    ProductInitial() => 'Initial',
    ProductLoading() => 'Loading',
    ProductLoaded(:final products) => '${products.length} products',
    ProductError(:final message) => 'Error: $message',
  };
}
```

The switch intentionally handles every known direct subtype.

## Step 3 — Add a New State

Add:

```dart
class ProductEmpty extends ProductState {}
```

Then observe the analyzer/compiler feedback for the existing exhaustive switch.

The new state demonstrates the main advantage of sealed hierarchies: code that claims to handle every state is forced to account for newly introduced variants.

## Step 4 — Base Class

```dart
base class DatabaseConnection {
  void connect() {
    print('Connecting');
  }
}
```

A subtype outside the defining library cannot implement the base class's interface independently.

## Step 5 — Interface Class

```dart
interface class PaymentGateway {
  void pay(double amount) {
    print('Payment');
  }
}
```

External code can implement it but cannot extend it.

## Step 6 — Final Class

```dart
final class Configuration {
  final String environment;

  Configuration(this.environment);
}
```

External code cannot create a subtype.

## Step 7 — Abstract Interface Class

```dart
abstract interface class ProductRepository {
  Future<List<String>> getProducts();
}
```

This defines an explicit non-instantiable contract.

## Step 8 — Flutter Integration

The sealed hierarchy can represent UI state:

```text
ProductState
├── Initial
├── Loading
├── Loaded
└── Error
```

A widget can switch over the state and produce the appropriate Material 3 UI for each variant.

The UI layer should remain focused on presentation while the state hierarchy models mutually exclusive application states.

## Step 9 — Production Extension

Convert the simple state model into a small product-list application using:

```text
Repository
↓
ViewModel / State Layer
↓
Sealed ProductState
↓
Flutter UI
```

The architecture should demonstrate why the sealed hierarchy is useful rather than merely demonstrating the syntax.
